  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 255 PM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A warming trend is expected through Thursday \nwith mostly dry conditions.\n\nAn upper ridge is building in from the W this afternoon as an \nupper trough lifts NE over Atlantic Canada. At the surface, high\npressure is centered near the VA/NC border. Mostly sunny with \ntemperatures in the lower to mid 80s. The wind is light, \ngenerally 5-10 mph, and ENE on the southern periphery of the \nhigh, and SSW on the northern side of the high. The surface high\nvery slowly slides offshore tonight, but lags back into \nS-central VA and interior NE NC. Therefore, low temperatures in \nthese areas potentially fall into the upper 50s/around 60F with \na calm to light wind under a clear sky. Farther N, low \ntemperatures are forecast to be 60-65F as very light southerly \nreturn flow develops.\n\nAn upper trough slides N of the Great Lakes Wednesday allowing \nthe upper ridge to flatten with more of a zonal flow pattern \ndeveloping. A a result, the surface high will slide farther \noffshore. A warming trend will commence. However, moisture \nrecovery will be slow. 850mb temperatures of 15-18C support high\ntemperatures in the upper 80s/near 90F inland, with mid 80s at \nthe coast. Mostly clear and milder Wednesday night with low \ntemperatures in the mid 60s to around 70F. The upper ridge \nbecomes further suppressed Thursday as more robust troughing \ndevelops over ON/QB, which will push a cold front SE through the\nGreat Lakes. 1000-850mb thicknesses surge ahead of the cold \nfront. Legacy 1000-850mb thicknesses schemes from the NAM/ECMWF \nsupport high temperatures in the lower/mid 90s, with mid/upper \n90s from the GFS. The current forecast is for lower to locally \nmid 90s. Dewpoints rise, but generally into the mid 60s to near \n70F. There is some uncertainty across the northern tier of the \narea as some guidance (NAM/RRFS) have some convection tracking \noff the northern Blue Ridge later Thursday afternoon. A SW wind \nof 10-15 mph with gusts up to 20 mph is expected to develop \nThursday afternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...An unsettled pattern develops late Thursday \ninto the weekend with daily chances for showers or \nthunderstorms.\n\nThe cold front likely slows down as it approaches from the N \nThursday afternoon as it becomes aligned parallel to the mid \nlevel flow. Most guidance has little to no QPF across the \nnorthern tier of the local area through 00z Friday, and the NAM \nand RRFS are outliers suggesting earlier convection. There is \nonly a thin strip of 20-30% PoPs across the northern tier of the\nlocal area 18z Thursday through 00z Friday. Otherwise, \nshowers/tstms are not expected during the day Thursday. The cold\nfront limps through the region Thursday night. The best chc of \nshowers/tstms will be across the northern tier of the area \nThursday evening into the early overnight hours (30-60% chc), \nwith mainly dry conditions across southern VA/NE NC. Given the \nlater timing and the better forcing remaining well to the N, the\npotential for any strong to severe tstms will be quite limited.\nA few additional showers/tstms are possible Friday across far \nsouthern portions of the area, but that will depend on how far \nsouth the cold front gets by early Friday. Behind the cold \nfront, CAA will be lacking so temperatures only cool back to \nnear normal Friday and Saturday with lower 90s potentially \nreturning by Sunday through Tuesday of next week. Some semblance\nof a front lingers over the area Saturday with chances of \nshowers/tstms persisting. This boundary gradually washes out \nSunday through Tuesday with PoPs diminishing Sunday, and \ngenerally 20% or less by Monday/Tuesday.",
